# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Pilot host environment — NAMES ONLY. Pilot Host Provisioning v1.
#
# Copy to /etc/sellerops/pilot.env on the host (owner root:root or the deploy
# user, mode 0600). Never commit the copy. Every connector below is OFF until an
# operator turns it on deliberately; a connector that is ON while its required
# values are blank is refused at boot (PilotConfigValidator) — that is the
# intended behaviour, not something to work around.
#
# Generate the two secrets on the host, never on a laptop that syncs to a cloud:
#   openssl rand -base64 48          → SELLEROPS_JWT_SECRET
#   openssl rand -base64 32          → SELLEROPS_VAULT_MASTER_KEY (AES-256)
#   openssl rand -base64 24 | tr -d '/+=' → POSTGRES_PASSWORD
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── The pilot this file is written for: CAFE24 ONLY (2026-09-13 decision) ────
# Cafe24 is the PRIMARY onboarding channel and the only one this pilot turns on.
# What that decides, so nobody spends a week on the wrong prerequisite:
#   • REQUIRED  a stable public HTTPS host name (PILOT_PUBLIC_HOST below) whose
#               A record resolves, with :80 and :443 reachable so ACME can issue.
#   • REQUIRED  a Cafe24 app whose registered redirect URI is byte-identical to
#               https://PILOT_PUBLIC_HOST/api/connect/cafe24/callback.
#   • NOT required  a fixed outbound IPv4. The advertised egress IP is NAVER's
#               requirement (its app refuses calls from an unregistered address);
#               Cafe24 calls out to nothing that checks our source address. An
#               Elastic IP is a prerequisite of ADDING NAVER, not of starting.
#               Cafe24ThenNaverPilotConfigTest proves the boot, both ways.
# Leave SELLEROPS_CONNECTOR_NAVER_ENABLED=false and the IP line blank.

# ── Public identity (product-owner input) ────────────────────────────────────
# The DNS name whose A record points at the Elastic IP. Used for TLS, CORS, the
# Cafe24 callback and the frontend's runtime URL. No scheme, no path.
PILOT_PUBLIC_HOST=
# Additional names the SAME site answers. QUOTED, and each name written as a comma, a SPACE, then
# the name:
#   (empty)                                    one name — the steady state
#   ", old.example.com"                        two names
#   ", old.example.com, older.example.com"     three
# Empty unless a hostname migration is in flight or a retired name must keep working. Both happen for
# the same reason: a redirect URI registered at Google, NAVER or Cafe24 is worthless until the name
# behind it serves HTTPS, and once registered it is not ours to retire on our own schedule — so the
# new name has to work BEFORE the switch and the old one has to keep working AFTER it.
#
# The exact punctuation matters and was measured against `caddy validate`, not assumed:
#   ", name"  correct
#   ",name"   REFUSED — the edge will not start and the site goes down, including the name that
#             was working a minute ago
#   "name"    parses SILENTLY into one concatenated host that resolves to nothing
# The quotes matter too: the value contains a space, and unquoted, bash reads the line as an
# assignment followed by a command (so preflight sees nothing) while docker compose still hands the
# whole string to Caddy. preflight.sh and deploy.sh refuse every other shape. Each name needs its own
# A record pointing here, and Caddy issues a separate certificate per name.
PILOT_EXTRA_HOSTS=
# Contact for certificate issuance notices (Let's Encrypt). ASCII ONLY — the issuer registers an
# ACCOUNT with this address before it will sign anything, and a non-ASCII character anywhere in it
# is refused outright (Let's Encrypt: `invalidContact`; ZeroSSL: `invalid_email` 2901). A stray IME
# character in front of the address is the easy way to do this, and it fails far from its cause:
# the deploy succeeds, every container is healthy, and the site simply never gets a certificate.
# preflight.sh checks this.
PILOT_ACME_EMAIL=

# ── Guided lanes on the seller's own machine (reviewnary 도우미) ─────────────
# `true` makes the pilot BUILD name the helper's local origin in its CSP, which is what lets the
# browser talk to the 도우미 at all — NAVER guided acquisition and Guided Reply composer fill.
# Left false, those screens keep working and degrade to the honest manual path (복사 → 판매자센터).
# The URL is the SELLER's loopback address, not this host's: the helper runs on their machine.
# The helper package must be built for THIS site (tools/helper/build-macos.sh REVIEWNARY_APP_URL /
# REVIEWNARY_BASE_URL) or its pairing will only accept a localhost origin.
PILOT_GUIDED_HELPER_ENABLED=false
PILOT_HELPER_BRIDGE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:47615

# ── Database (container on this host, named volume; see backup.sh) ───────────
POSTGRES_DB=sellerops
POSTGRES_USER=sellerops
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# Forward-only schema: there is no undo script for any migration and none will be
# written after the fact — rollback is the pre-migration dump deploy.sh takes.
# `false` means a non-empty database with no Flyway history FAILS the boot rather
# than being assumed current, which is the shape a partial restore leaves behind.
# deploy.sh refuses any other value here.
SELLEROPS_FLYWAY_BASELINE_ON_MIGRATE=false

# ── Off-host backup (blocker B5) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# A host-local dump does not survive the host, which is the only failure this
# exists for. `true` makes deploy/pilot/backup.sh copy each daily dump to
# S3-compatible object storage with ONE PutObject call, and a dump that could
# not be copied off the host is reported as a FAILURE — not a success with a
# warning. These are read by backup.sh on the HOST (cron), never by a container.
#
# REQUIRED ON A PILOT HOST (2026-09-26). `deploy.sh` REFUSES to deploy while this
# is not `true`, while the bucket or the region below is blank, or while the aws
# cli is missing; `preflight.sh` scores each of those as a failure rather than a
# note. The value stays `false` in this example because the destination below is
# blank and a `true` with blanks fails for a less useful reason — fill it in, set
# this to `true`, and run deploy/pilot/install-backup-job.sh, all BEFORE the
# first deploy. What the refusal prevents is not "no backups": the local dump is
# written either way. It is a host beginning to accumulate seller data it cannot
# recover.
SELLEROPS_BACKUP_S3_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=
# Required even for an endpoint that has no regions: the SigV4 signer needs a
# value, and a silent default is a signature mismatch found at 03:17 by nobody.
SELLEROPS_BACKUP_S3_REGION=
# Only for non-AWS S3-compatible storage. Blank = AWS S3.
SELLEROPS_BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT=
# PUT-ONLY CREDENTIAL. Grant s3:PutObject on this bucket and nothing else — no
# DeleteObject, no ListBucket, no multipart. backup.sh needs nothing more, and a
# host that cannot delete is a host that cannot erase its own backup history.
# Retention belongs to the BUCKET'S LIFECYCLE POLICY (30 days), never to this
# credential and never to the script.
#
# THE PAIR IS A THREE-STATE SETTING, and blank is a posture rather than a gap:
#   both set   — an explicit long-lived key pair, handed to the AWS CLI through
#                its own environment and never written to a profile or a command
#                line. Use this off AWS, or against S3-compatible storage.
#   both blank — nothing is handed over, and the CLI resolves the grant through
#                its DEFAULT CREDENTIAL PROVIDER CHAIN. On an EC2 host that is
#                the INSTANCE ROLE: issued to the instance, rotated for it, and
#                impossible to copy off the host by reading this file. THIS IS
#                THE SHADOW-RUN POSTURE — no long-lived access key is created.
#   one of two — REFUSED by deploy.sh, scored a failure by preflight.sh, and a
#                hard failure in backup.sh. It is never a third mode, only a
#                half-finished edit, and the alternative to refusing it is a
#                SigV4 error at 03:17 that nobody is awake to read.
# In the blank/blank case the two AWS_* variables are not exported to the CLI AT
# ALL — not even as empty strings, because a set-but-empty credential is found by
# the provider chain first and shadows the role — and AWS_EC2_METADATA_DISABLED
# is left unset, since that switch is precisely what would turn the role off.
SELLEROPS_BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
SELLEROPS_BACKUP_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
#
# NOT IN THIS FILE, AND THAT IS THE POINT: the off-host copy of
# SELLEROPS_VAULT_MASTER_KEY. The dump holds sealed credentials and no key, so a
# dump restored under a different key opens nothing. Keep that key somewhere
# OTHER than this bucket — if one place holds both the ciphertext and the key,
# losing that place loses both. Nothing here can check it; the restore rehearsal
# on a NEW host is what proves it (Pilot Provisioning Plan v1 §6-B).

# ── Backend ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SELLEROPS_JWT_SECRET=
SELLEROPS_JWT_EXP_MINUTES=720
# Optional JVM override; the overlay's default is -Xms256m -Xmx1024m for a 4 GB host.
# SELLEROPS_JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS=

# ── Seller sign-in with Google / NAVER (optional) ────────────────────────────
# The SELLER'S LOGIN. Not the NAVER commerce connector below — different console,
# different credential, different callback. A provider with both values gets a
# button; a provider with neither does not exist (no endpoint, no button), so
# leaving these blank is a supported posture: email + password still works.
# Register this exact callback in each console (Spring's default template):
#   Google : https://PILOT_PUBLIC_HOST/login/oauth2/code/google
#   NAVER  : https://PILOT_PUBLIC_HOST/login/oauth2/code/naver
SELLEROPS_OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
SELLEROPS_OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
SELLEROPS_OAUTH_NAVER_CLIENT_ID=
SELLEROPS_OAUTH_NAVER_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── Demo fixture: OFF on any host a real seller can reach ────────────────────
SELLEROPS_SEED_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_SEED_DEMO_CONTENT=false
SELLEROPS_SEED_DEMO_ENTRY=false

# ── Credential vault (required before ANY connector is on) ───────────────────
SELLEROPS_VAULT_MASTER_KEY=
SELLEROPS_VAULT_KEY_ID=pilot-v1
# Retired keys, so a rotation can still open rows sealed by the previous one.
# Format: "id:base64,id:base64". Empty until the first rotation — and it must be filled BEFORE
# changing SELLEROPS_VAULT_KEY_ID, or every stored credential reads KEY_MISMATCH.
SELLEROPS_VAULT_KEY_RING=

# Password reset mail. `off` sends nothing (the seller asks an operator). `smtp` needs the SMTP
# settings; `dev-outbox` writes the whole message — reset link included — into the log and is
# refused by deploy.sh on a pilot host.
SELLEROPS_MAIL_MODE=off

# A channel whose connector is off must collect NOTHING. Both default to false;
# they are written out here so a pilot host states the fence rather than inherits
# it, and so flipping one on is a visible edit to this file.
SELLEROPS_CONNECTOR_MOCK_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_CONNECTOR_MOCK_FALLBACK_ENABLED=false

# ── Connectors — turn on one at a time, after its external registration ──────
# NAVER: the advertised egress IP is THIS host's Elastic IP, verified by
# deploy/pilot/egress-check.sh before the flag is flipped.
SELLEROPS_CONNECTOR_NAVER_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_CONNECTOR_NAVER_ADVERTISED_EGRESS_IPS=
# The two NAVER 문의 lanes have their own flags — the connector flag above does not turn them on.
# They are separate official contracts with non-overlapping identifier spaces, so there are two.
SELLEROPS_CONNECTOR_NAVER_INQUIRY_PRODUCT_QNA_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_CONNECTOR_NAVER_INQUIRY_CUSTOMER_ENABLED=false
# Coupang: keys are entered by the seller in the product; nothing here but the flag and the
# operator id Coupang requires on an answer — blank and the answer adapter refuses to send.
SELLEROPS_CONNECTOR_COUPANG_ENABLED=
# Coupang INQUIRY read, with NO Self-Pilot and NO collect poller: an organisation-scoped standing READ
# grant. Both blank = nothing admitted. `*` is refused on the list; a malformed id refuses the boot.
# Turn the connector flag on BEFORE the account is connected — collectable() answers true when a channel
# has no collector at all, so an account connected while the connector is off keeps the responsibility
# source and records CONNECTOR_UNAVAILABLE in every window.
SELLEROPS_CONNECTOR_COUPANG_INQUIRY_READ_GRANT_ID=
SELLEROPS_CONNECTOR_COUPANG_INQUIRY_READ_GRANT_ORG_IDS=false
SELLEROPS_CONNECTOR_COUPANG_REPLY_BY=
# Cafe24: app credentials from Cafe24 Developers. REDIRECT_URI / RESULT_URL default to
# https://PILOT_PUBLIC_HOST/api/connect/cafe24/callback and /connect/cafe24/result in the
# overlay — the first must be byte-identical to the URI registered in the Cafe24 app.
SELLEROPS_CONNECTOR_CAFE24_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_CONNECTOR_CAFE24_CLIENT_ID=
SELLEROPS_CONNECTOR_CAFE24_CLIENT_SECRET=
SELLEROPS_CONNECTOR_CAFE24_API_VERSION=
# SELLEROPS_CONNECTOR_CAFE24_REDIRECT_URI=
# SELLEROPS_CONNECTOR_CAFE24_RESULT_URL=
# The separate re-consent scope an answer WRITE needs (mall.write_community). Blank keeps the
# connection read-only; the seller grants it themselves, and only then can a reply be posted.
SELLEROPS_CONNECTOR_CAFE24_ANSWER_EXECUTION_SCOPES=

# ── Routine acquisition: every CONNECTED seller, read from the DB ─────────────
SELLEROPS_SELF_PILOT_ENABLED=true
SELLEROPS_SELF_PILOT_SCOPE=CONNECTED_SELLERS
SELLEROPS_SELF_PILOT_DEFAULT_INTERVAL_MINUTES=60
# Self-pilot CREATES the schedules; this poller EXECUTES them. Both halves are needed for routine
# collection — with this false, schedules appear and nothing ever collects.
#
# It shipped `false` while the line above said `true`, and the pairing is the whole point: Cafe24
# reaches CONNECTED on the OAuth callback alone — there is no first-sync button anywhere in that
# flow — and the result screen then tells the seller 「이제 문의·리뷰·주문이 자동으로 수집됩니다」.
# On the Cafe24 Inquiry pilot (Pilot Readiness v3 §3) that sentence has to be true, so this is now
# `true`, and deploy.sh / preflight.sh refuse the half-on combination rather than leaving a seller to
# discover it by waiting for data that is not coming. `false` was the right value for the earlier
# Coupang browser-only lane, which had no API collection at all.
SELLEROPS_COLLECT_SCHEDULER_ENABLED=true
# Standing READ grant for Coupang signed GETs (spr-<hex>), READ only.
SELLEROPS_SELF_PILOT_READ_GRANT_ID=

# ── Agent AI capabilities: the /agent lane. Keys are per-capability ──────────
# PLAN off ⇒ every free-language Agent turn fails by contract (no fallback
# planner exists). CONNECTED_SELLERS is what makes a new pilot seller usable
# without an env edit or a restart: connecting a channel IS the admission.
# It covers PLAN / DRAFT / JUDGE / CONVERSE / REPORT and NOT the three knowledge-retrieval
# capabilities further down, which are admitted by name only.
SELLEROPS_AGENT_ACCESS_SCOPE=CONNECTED_SELLERS
SELLEROPS_AGENT_PLAN_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_AGENT_PLAN_API_KEY=
SELLEROPS_AGENT_DRAFT_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_AGENT_DRAFT_API_KEY=
SELLEROPS_AGENT_JUDGE_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_AGENT_JUDGE_API_KEY=
SELLEROPS_AGENT_CONVERSE_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_AGENT_CONVERSE_API_KEY=

# The period report capability. deploy.sh validates these three and the template did not mention
# them, so an operator turning it on had no name to copy.
SELLEROPS_AGENT_REPORT_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_AGENT_REPORT_API_KEY=
SELLEROPS_AGENT_REPORT_ORG_IDS=
# The daily ceiling ENFORCES on a pilot — that is the default and it is left alone here on purpose.
# Only local/QA/benchmark deployments set SELLEROPS_AGENT_QUOTA_ENFORCED=false, which keeps the meter
# running and stops the refusals.

# ── Inquiry AI capabilities. Same three-way switch, one per exposure ────────
# GOAL interprets what the seller asked for; DECISION plans and judges an answer; SIGNATURE classifies
# an inquiry. GOAL and DECISION carry a customer's own sentence and therefore do NOT ride on
# SELLEROPS_AGENT_ACCESS_SCOPE above — each needs the pilot organisation named in its own list, which
# is the same two-deploy order the knowledge block below spells out.
SELLEROPS_INQUIRY_GOAL_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_INQUIRY_GOAL_API_KEY=
SELLEROPS_INQUIRY_GOAL_ORG_IDS=
SELLEROPS_INQUIRY_DECISION_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_INQUIRY_DECISION_API_KEY=
SELLEROPS_INQUIRY_DECISION_ORG_IDS=
SELLEROPS_INQUIRY_SIGNATURE_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_INQUIRY_SIGNATURE_API_KEY=
SELLEROPS_INQUIRY_SIGNATURE_ORG_IDS=

# ── Review AI triage: the 「AI 확인 필요」 mark, and what makes it appear ──────
# A customer's review text leaves. Two switches, and only both together mean 「the list marks itself」:
# the pilot classifies when asked, AUTO classifies on collection. Without AUTO the review screen still
# works — it shows the rules-only tier, which needs no key and no vendor.
SELLEROPS_AI_TRIAGE_PILOT_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_AI_TRIAGE_PILOT_ORG_IDS=
SELLEROPS_AI_TRIAGE_API_KEY=
SELLEROPS_SELF_PILOT_TRIAGE_AUTO_ENABLED=false
# ── Semantic knowledge retrieval: NAMED ORGANISATIONS ONLY ───────────────────
# These three send the CUSTOMER'S QUESTION to a vendor — on searches and drafts that call no model
# today — so ACCESS_SCOPE above does NOT widen them (Pilot Release Closure v1 §2). Connecting a
# channel admits a seller to the Agent; it does not make them the subject of these calls. Turning
# one on means: flag + key + the pilot organisation's UUID written into its own list. On + keyed +
# blank list is refused at boot, deliberately, rather than running silently disabled.
#
# Product-owner decision 2026-09-18 (Customer Ops Demo Closure v1): the first pilot runs WITH all three
# (embedding + question restatement + evidence relevance check) for the pilot allow-list; the global
# default in application.yml stays OFF. Measured on the Inquiry Quality Set: false grounding 3 → 0,
# wrong rule 1 → 0. That decision stands and is TURNED ON BELOW, in the second of two deploys.
#
# ── THIS TEMPLATE SHIPS THEM OFF, AND THAT IS THE ORDER, NOT A RETREAT ───────
# The template used to ship `true` with blank lists, and a host copying it could not complete its
# FIRST deploy: `on + keyed + blank list` is refused — deliberately, by deploy.sh AND by the backend's
# own boot validator — and the UUID that would satisfy it belongs to an organisation that does not
# exist yet, because signing up requires this stack to be serving. On + blank was unreachable and
# unrefusable at the same time.
#
# Neither refusal is relaxed. Running a vendor capability that is switched on and reaches nobody, with
# nothing said, is the defect they were both written for. What changes is the starting value, so the
# shipped template is deployable as copied:
#
#   1. deploy with these three false  →  the stack serves
#   2. sign up in the browser, read the organisation's UUID   (runbook §7-0 steps 1–2)
#   3. write the UUID into all three lists, set the keys, set all three true   (§7-0 step 3)
#   4. deploy again — restart #1 of the two the runbook budgets for
#
# Until step 3 an inquiry draft is grounded by the lexical retriever, which needs no key and no vendor.
SELLEROPS_KNOWLEDGE_EMBEDDING_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_KNOWLEDGE_EMBEDDING_API_KEY=
SELLEROPS_KNOWLEDGE_EMBEDDING_ORG_IDS=
# Retrieval intent (restates the customer's sentence) and evidence eligibility (a refusal-only
# judge after ranking, the widest payload of the three: the customer's words next to the seller's
# own text). Each is its own exposure, its own key, its own list.
SELLEROPS_KNOWLEDGE_INTENT_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_KNOWLEDGE_INTENT_API_KEY=
SELLEROPS_KNOWLEDGE_INTENT_ORG_IDS=
SELLEROPS_KNOWLEDGE_ELIGIBILITY_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_KNOWLEDGE_ELIGIBILITY_API_KEY=
SELLEROPS_KNOWLEDGE_ELIGIBILITY_ORG_IDS=
# Review-photo vision: a customer's own photo leaves. Not part of the pilot decision above — OFF.
SELLEROPS_REVIEW_MEDIA_VISION_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_REVIEW_MEDIA_VISION_API_KEY=
SELLEROPS_REVIEW_MEDIA_VISION_ORG_IDS=

# ── Proactive Agent: a SEPARATE decision. Stays OFF for the first sellers. ───
SELLEROPS_PROACTIVE_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_PROACTIVE_ORG_IDS=

# ── Responsibility Runtime (「고객 운영 관리」): a SEPARATE decision. Acts only for sellers who activated it. ─
SELLEROPS_RESPONSIBILITY_SCHEDULER_ENABLED=false
# Rollout allow-list — explicit pilot organisation UUIDs only. Blank = nobody.
RESPONSIBILITY_RUNTIME_ORG_IDS=
# Case investigator (model calls on redacted customer text): its own decision.
SELLEROPS_RESPONSIBILITY_INVESTIGATION_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_RESPONSIBILITY_INVESTIGATION_API_KEY=
SELLEROPS_RESPONSIBILITY_INVESTIGATION_ORG_IDS=

# ── Unattended NAVER review export (72h shadow run): a SEPARATE decision ─────
# Normally a review-import launch is minted only by the seller's own session, and that one press
# IS the human checkpoint for a bounded browser export (PD-7). These three remove it for ONE
# organisation and ONE paired device, for acquisition only, and for nothing else.
#
# NEITHER LIST ACCEPTS `*` — there is no posture in which "every organisation" or "every device"
# is the right answer for driving a seller centre with nobody present. deploy.sh refuses a `*`
# and refuses ENABLED=true with either list blank.
#
# Both ids exist only after the fact, so this is a SECOND deploy pass:
#   1. deploy with ENABLED=false, sign up, connect NAVER  -> organisation UUID
#   2. pair the agent VM (POST /api/auth/device/code -> approve in the browser -> /token),
#      read its id from GET /api/helper-devices          -> device UUID
#   3. set all three, deploy again
SELLEROPS_REVIEW_IMPORT_UNATTENDED_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_REVIEW_IMPORT_UNATTENDED_ORG_IDS=
SELLEROPS_REVIEW_IMPORT_UNATTENDED_DEVICE_IDS=

# ── Answer execution (marketplace WRITE): OFF until its own approval ─────────
SELLEROPS_INQUIRY_PUBLISH_EXECUTION_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_INQUIRY_PUBLISH_NAVER_LIVE_APPROVAL_ID=
SELLEROPS_INQUIRY_PUBLISH_CAFE24_LIVE_APPROVAL_ID=
# The Cafe24 reply request's client_ip is deployment config: this host's Elastic IP.
SELLEROPS_INQUIRY_PUBLISH_CAFE24_CLIENT_IP=
SELLEROPS_INQUIRY_PUBLISH_CAFE24_SHOP_NO=
# Review replies are a SECOND execution lane with its own flag and its own approval id — the inquiry
# names above do not reach it. Cafe24 is the only channel with a review-reply WRITE adapter
# (board comment, IMPLEMENTED · never posted to a real mall); NAVER review replies are the guided
# composer, which has no flag here, and Coupang has no seller reply feature at all.
# SELLEROPS_REVIEW_PUBLISH_CAFE24_SHOP_NO is deliberately absent: it inherits the inquiry shop number
# above unless a deployment genuinely needs a different shop.
SELLEROPS_REVIEW_PUBLISH_EXECUTION_ENABLED=false
SELLEROPS_REVIEW_PUBLISH_CAFE24_LIVE_APPROVAL_ID=

# ── Agent runtime (lifecycle owned by compose; production mode, backend store) ─
AGENT_RUNTIME_APP_ENV=production
AGENT_RUNTIME_RUNSTORE_KIND=spring
